  ratio of per-aggregate units 150 C / 152 C: 42x
P4a (derived): smallest possible n_eff from any resample = 33.8 (max ee^2 0.0296); bootstrap (20000, seed 0) 2.5th percentile n_eff = 104.1 vs chi-square low end 88.3; both far above 2.4

=== Asakura 2002: pre-registered predictions (blind_units_posthoc_predictions.txt), scored by the fixed rules ===
  Rnuc(150 C) = 1/(979 s x 3.14 cm^2) = 0.000325 cm^-2 s^-1 (printed 0.000325; txt:168-172)
  Rnuc(152 C) = 1/(3150 s x 3.14 cm^2) = 0.000101 cm^-2 s^-1 (printed 0.000101; txt:168-172)
  Rnuc ratio 150/152 = 3.22x; units per aggregate need ~42x; visible aggregates 24 vs 32 (txt:191-194)
  quote-check lines (txt): {'cannot really count the number of crystals': 185, 'recurrence of nucleations on or near the surface': 190, '20 ml of benzene': 121, '7 out of the 10 crystallizations': 128, 'polycrystalline aggregates consisting of both R- and S-': 155, 'very small R- and S-crystals': 275, 'large number of lattice': 238, 'tallites with many lattice defects': 307, '0.50 g': 105, 'mostly racemic': 129, 'ee as high as 17%': 248}
  P1 (E1: paper describes merging of separately nucleated masses at 150 C): NO.  Nearest text says new nuclei form near a crystal and join the growing solid (txt:183-185), i.e. secondary nucleation.  E1 does not survive.
  P2a (150 C solid = many small crystallites of both hands within a mass): HELD.  Discussion: at high supercooling the solid is a polycrystalline aggregate of very small R and S crystals (txt:275); the stirred-crystallization section allows either many small single-handed crystals or crystals with many lattice defects taking up both hands (txt:238-240); the paper does not choose.
  P2b (independent crystallite count >= 37 per aggregate or 88 per run): NOT TESTABLE.  No count or size at 150 C; Fig. 1 is 152 C only, rings 1.0 mm apart, 'cloudy' (txt:77-81); the paper says the crystals cannot really be counted (txt:185).
  P2c (faster or finer at 150 C): HELD in direction (first nucleus 979 s vs 3,150 s, Rnuc 3.2x); the size of the effect is not matched.
  P3 (ee method and precision <= +-5 points): method found (JASCO polarimeter, the solid 'thus obtained' dissolved in 20 ml benzene, 1.0 dm cell, txt:120-122; no subsampling mentioned); precision NOT stated: NOT TESTABLE as worded.  The only route for E3 (aliquot averaging) is absent, so E3 does not survive.
  P4b (paper's fitted low-stirring distribution SD <= 10.7 points): NOT TESTABLE; the paper gives the Langevin form and no fitted values.  (Its printed SD, 6% at 150 C, is the data blind_units.py already reproduced: blind_units.py:132.)
  Survival by the fixed rule: E1 no; E2 YES (P2a, P2c HELD, none FAILED); E3 no; E4 not supported (P4a is derived from used data, not an unused item).
  Reading: the visible aggregate is not the unit, so 'n_eff 272' cannot be compared with 2.4 aggregates.  The paper does not support 'n_eff counts independent crystallites': it counts none, and the near-zero ee of 150 C masses is also what lattice-defect incorporation of both hands would give (X3).  What E2 does not explain: the 42x per-aggregate jump over 2 K, and 7 of 10 runs at exactly 0 (X1).

